Z||000000||Monkeyman||09-29-2000||07:13 PM||||I found an unusual quarter today.  It's <B>VERY</B> worn, but some of the markings are still visible.  The front has a picture of someone standing with a bag over his shoulder?  Has "LIBERTY" across the top, split by the pictures head.  "IN GOD WE TRUST" is across the middle, split by the same unknown figure.  Looks like 6 stars down the left side and 5 stars down the right side of the figure.  The back has a flying eagle, wings forming a "V".  Instead of the "UNITED STATES OF AMERICA" being in a single line (as on the current quarters), this one has "UNITED STATES" on the top with "OF" under that, and "AMERICA" under that.  I'm sure it's just old and not worth much, but I'd be interested in finding out something about it.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||09-29-2000||07:51 PM||||Like any of these?<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/coin1_1.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/coin2.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/coin3.jpg"> <P>(Notice the small differences)<P>It's a quarter dollar from somewhere between 1916-1930. This type quarter was designed by Herman A. MacNeil. The left arm of Liberty is upraised uncovering a shield in the attitude of protection. Her right hand bears the olive branch of peace. MacNeil's initial M is located above and to the right of the date. There was a modification in 1917 because of public resentment to Liberty's exposed breast. The reverse has a new arrangement of the stars and the eagle is higher. In 1925 a depression was made in the pedestal on which Liberty stands and which bears the date. On the first issues the dates wore off easily because they were too high and were not protected by other features of the coin. The new "recessed" dates proved more durable as a result of this change. No proof coins of this type were officially issued, but specimen strikings of the first variety, dated 1917, are known to exist. Designed by Hermon A. MacNeil. Standards same as previous issue. Minted at Philadelphia, Denver, and San Francisco.<P>Anything else?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cliff Pennock||09-29-2000||08:12 PM||||PS: It's a pretty rare find. It could be worth anywhere between $10-$50,000 depending on the year and quality...||||reg||1
Z||000003||cobra427||09-30-2000||12:36 AM||||cliff, I think that your $50,000 is a little stretched.The book that I have is three years old and it lists the 1918s over 7 in unc condition as only being a little less than $10,000. And that is the most valuable coin in that era, with the 1916 and 1927s as being next in line as to value.(unc condition). But then again, maybe they haved jumped to what you stated in the passed three years. Unlikely but possible the way things are going these days<P>cobra||||reg||1
Z||000004||RossT||09-30-2000||02:22 AM||||MM.  I would sell the quater and buy another fiero or two...||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||09-30-2000||05:34 AM||||You hit the nail on the head, Cliff. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  One of those appears to be exactly what I have.  Mine would probably be worth more towards the lower end, though as it's far, far from "uncirculated condition".  I'm guessing it's towards the earlier date as it only seems to have 5 stars on the right side.  (I'm just guessing that more stars equals a later date.)  I wonder who I could take this to who could give me an exact value (and possibly purchase it).<P>Cliff--How did you get <B>3</B> of those old quarters?  Seems like it would be easier to get them here in the states and I've never even seen one (lived here all my life).||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||09-30-2000||06:09 AM||||MM, I'm not a coin-collector (so I don't have those coins), but when it comes to searching stuff on the net, I'm your man.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Anyway, the date should be on the pedestal where liberty stands on. If there's no date visible check the backside below the eagle. Does it have the three stars?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||09-30-2000||02:57 PM||||It's got the 3 stars under the eagle (so, if your pics are in chronological order from oldest to newest, mine would be an "newer" quarter) but the date has been worn off of the front.  It has a small "M" stamped in the front, lower, right side.  Probably is worth closer to $10 than $50k since it's in decidedly poor condition, but I'm sure it must be worth something.||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||10-01-2000||09:46 PM||||Monkey, sorry to disappoint you, but if the date is worn completely off so that you can't even read it at all, then it's not worth anything but the value of the silver (it's 90% fine). You'd be hard pressed to find anyone to pay more than a couple of bucks for it. I've got several of those with worn off dates too (but a couple good ones too), it was poor design for a coin. Buffalo nickels are another one that is notorious for wearing the date off rapidly.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Songman||10-03-2000||11:51 PM||||I've never even seen one of those! Very cool no matter what the value or condition!||||reg||14
